      "math_academy_id": "q-213298",
      "sequence_position": 4,
      "problem": "Consider the following relation defined on the set $A={0,1,2}.$ \n\n$$\nR={(0,0),(0,1),(1,1),(1,2),(2,1)}\n$$\n\nSelect the options that make the following reasoning correct:\n\nSince \n{{field-1}}\nand\n{{field-2}}, \nthe relation is \n{{field-3}}.\n\nSince \n{{field-4}}\nand\n{{field-5}}, \nthe relation is \n{{field-6}}.",
      "worked_solution": "First, recall the following definitions:\n\n- A binary relation $R,$ defined on a set $A,$ is *symmetric* if for all $x,y\\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\,⟹\\,(y,x)\\in R.\n$$\n\nIn other words, if a pair $(x,y)$ belongs to a symmetric relation, then the pair $(y,x)$ should also be in the relation.\n\n- A binary relation $R,$ defined on a set $A,$ is *antisymmetric* if for all $x,y\\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\,\\text{and}\\,(y,x)\\in R\\,⟹\\,x=y.\n$$\n\nIn other words, only one of the pairs, either $(x,y)$ or $(y,x),$ can be in an antisymmetric relation. Note that this condition allows pairs of the form $(x,x).$\n\nOur relation is defined on ${0,1,2}.$ Notice that:\n\n- $(0,1)\\in R$ and $(1,0)\\notin R.$ So, the relation is $\\text{not symmetric}.$\n\n- $(1,2)\\in R$ and $(2,1)\\in R.$ So, the relation is $\\text{not antisymmetric}.$\n\nTherefore, we can conclude that the relation is neither symmetric nor antisymmetric.",
